{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y5KtvwmbYvzLdAGB1CaABn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for a Dream simulation based on their research project \"causal_style_decomp_judge\" — specifically the D015 framing revision about \"The Likert Blindspot.\"\n\nLet me analyze the existing data and experimental plan carefully:\n\n**Current state:**\n- Formality pairwise (GPT-4o × LLMBar): 69.1% formal win rate, p=0.006 ✅\n- Likert ATE on same data: p=0.332 (non-significant) ✅\n- This gives the core C2 \"Likert Blindspot\" finding\n\n\n- Verbosity and tone dimensions haven't met the 80% NLI threshold yet, and I'm still working with just one judge and one benchmark so far.\n\nThe critical next steps are running the binarized Likert sign test for C2, expanding to multiple judges and benchmarks, and validating the preference control across different conditions before scaling up to n=500.\n\nI'm tracking seven key metrics across the experiment tiers: whether the binarized Likert test shows significance, multi-judge win rates and p-values, multi-benchmark results, how many judge-benchmark combinations show significant pairwise differences, whether the Likert-Pairwise gap holds up across conditions, the magnitude of the original preference confound, and ultimately the paper score targeting best≥4.0 and median≥3.5.\n\nFor the best case scenario, I need the binarized Likert sign test to come back non-significant—that would confirm C2 is about measurement method differences rather than just test selection—plus at least two judges showing significant formality bias in pairwise comparisons, with the Likert-Pairwise gap persisting across judges.\n\nIn the median case, the binarized Likert result would be borderline, meaning C2 needs qualification with language like \"in most conditions\" rather than claiming systematic effects, and I'd expect only 1-2 judges to show significance.\n\nThe worst case is if binarized Likert comes back significant, which would falsify C2 as stated, or if only GPT-4o shows the effect across judges—then I'd need to reframe the finding around pairwise being more statistically powerful rather than a fundamental measurement difference.\n\nNow I'm defining the specific metrics I'll track to assess these scenarios: the p-value from the binarized Likert experiment, how many judges replicate the pairwise formality bias, how many benchmarks show the effect, the range of formal win rates across judges, whether the Likert/Pairwise gap pattern holds consistently, how much original preference explains the effect, and what review score I'd expect based on the results.\n\nLooking at the pairwise_32b data, I'm noticing something important — the 69.1% formal win rate is heavily driven by cases where formality and original preference align (78.8% in that subset), while the casual-original subset shows almost no bias at 54.5%. This suggests the effect isn't purely about formality but about preference alignment, which is a meaningful limitation to address in the framing.\n\nNow I'm setting up the tier structure to reflect this nuance. The best-case scenario requires the casual-original subset to show significant formality bias independently, ruling out the original preference confound, plus replication across multiple benchmarks. The median tier allows for borderline Likert results and weaker casual-original performance, but requires qualification about the interaction effect. The worst case is when the Likert becomes significant, suggesting the core claim breaks down, or when only one judge shows the effect, or when the casual-original subset never reaches significance — any of which would mean the formality bias story collapses into an original preference story instead.\n\nI'm now translating this into YAML with ranges for formal win rates and p-values across judges. I'm working through the three outcome scenarios for the casual-original win rate—best case where it improves to 60-65% and reaches significance with more judges, median case where it stays weak but the overall effect holds, and worst case where it remains flat across conditions, suggesting the formality advantage is mostly driven by original preference bias. Now I need to map these against the projected review scores, keeping in mind the user's targets of 4.0+ for best tier and 3.5+ for median tier on what appears to be a 1-5 or 1-6 scale.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12716,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12716},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9da4503e-2b61-45f1-8689-f637435f2d27","uuid":"8fd417c7-81c5-4b36-b735-89340c9fb61b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y5KtvwmbYvzLdAGB1CaABn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"C2 完全确认 + 多 judge 泛化 + original preference 可分离 → Main competitive\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"exp-010 binarized Likert sign test 不显著 (p>0.10) → Likert Blindspot 是测量方法结构性差异，非统计检验选择差异\"\n      - \"exp-001 至少 2/3 judges (含 GPT-4o) pairwise formality bias 显著 (p<0.05 after Holm correction)\"\n      - \"exp-002 至少 2/3 benchmarks 复制 Likert-Pairwise gap\"\n      - \"exp-005 casual-original subset 在 ≥1 judge 上 formal win rate ≥60% 且 p<0.10 → formality effect 不完全被 original preference 解释\"\n      - \"exp-014 logistic regression 中 formality 主效应显著 (p<0.05)，interaction term 不显著或弱\"\n      - \"exp-006 n=500 复制 p<0.01\"\n    number_ranges:\n      binarized_likert_sign_p: [0.12, 1.0]\n      n_judges_pairwise_significant: [2, 3]\n      n_benchmarks_with_gap: [2, 3]\n      cross_judge_formal_win_rate: [0.60, 0.75]\n      cross_judge_pairwise_p_median: [0.001, 0.02]\n      cross_judge_likert_ate_p_median: [0.15, 0.50]\n      casual_orig_formal_win_rate_best_judge: [0.60, 0.70]\n      logistic_formality_coef_p: [0.001, 0.05]\n      n500_formal_win_rate: [0.58, 0.70]\n      n500_pairwise_p: [0.0001, 0.005]\n      nli_threshold_sensitivity_significant_range: [\"70%-95%\"]\n      projected_review_score: [4.0, 4.5]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      原始 \"Likert Blindspot\" framing 完全成立。C1 (application) + C2 (primary, \n      binarized Likert 控制确认) + C3 (preliminary, formality robust + tone/verbosity \n      boundaries) + C4 (practitioner guidance with CSD-adjusted win rate)。\n      Headline: \"Standard Likert scoring misses style bias that pairwise comparison \n      detects across N judges and M benchmarks — a structural measurement limitation, \n      not a statistical artifact.\" \n      Main result table 中 binarized Likert 列提供决定性证据。\n      Original preference 通过 logistic regression 正式分离，formality 主效应独立显著。\n      Claims 无需调整，25+ refs 到位，直冲 Main。\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"C2 部分确认（binarized Likert 模糊）+ 泛化有限 + original preference 纠缠未解 → Findings solid\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"exp-010 binarized Likert sign test 边界结果 (p=0.04-0.10) → 不能完全排除统计检验差异解释，但也不能确认\"\n      - \"exp-001 仅 1-2/3 judges 显著；至少 1 judge (如 Gemini) pairwise 也不显著\"\n      - \"exp-002 仅 1/3 additional benchmark 复制 gap（另一个 judge-specific 或 benchmark 特性导致不复制）\"\n      - \"casual-original subset 在所有 judges 上均 50-56% (p>0.20) → formality effect 与 original preference 无法分离\"\n      - \"exp-014 logistic regression 中 formality×original interaction 显著 → effect conditional on original preference alignment\"\n      - \"exp-006 n=500 复制 overall effect (p<0.05) 但 casual-original subset 仍不显著\"\n    number_ranges:\n      binarized_likert_sign_p: [0.04, 0.12]\n      n_judges_pairwise_significant: [1, 2]\n      n_benchmarks_with_gap: [1, 2]\n      cross_judge_formal_win_rate: [0.55, 0.68]\n      cross_judge_pairwise_p_median: [0.01, 0.08]\n      cross_judge_likert_ate_p_median: [0.10, 0.40]\n      casual_orig_formal_win_rate_best_judge: [0.50, 0.58]\n      logistic_formality_coef_p: [0.05, 0.20]\n      logistic_interaction_p: [0.01, 0.10]\n      n500_formal_win_rate: [0.55, 0.63]\n      n500_pairwise_p: [0.005, 0.05]\n      projected_review_score: [3.3, 3.8]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      C2 需要 hedge：\"Likert scoring shows reduced sensitivity to style bias \n      compared to pairwise comparison, though the source of this gap — measurement \n      structure vs. statistical power — remains partially ambiguous.\" \n      Binarized Likert 结果放在 discussion 中作为 open question 而非 main claim 的证据。\n      Original preference confound 成为论文最大 limitation — 诚实承认 \n      \"observed formality preference may partly reflect original-text preference \n      correlated with formality\" 并展示 logistic regression interaction。\n      C1 保持（method works），C3 保持（preliminary），C4 调整为 \n      \"use pairwise for auditing; interpret with caution when original preference \n      aligns with style axis\"。\n      标题可保留 \"Likert Blindspot\" 但需在 abstract 加 qualifier。\n      泛化叙事从 \"across judges and benchmarks\" 降级为 \n      \"demonstrated on GPT-4o with partial replication on [other judge]\"。\n      仍投 EMNLP Main，预期 Findings accept。\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"C2 被证伪（binarized Likert 也显著）或效应不泛化 → 需要 reframe 但仍冲 EMNLP\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"exp-010 binarized Likert sign test 显著 (p<0.04) → Likert-Pairwise gap 来自 sign test vs paired t-test 的统计功效差异，非测量方法结构性差异\"\n      - \"OR exp-001 仅 GPT-4o 显著，Claude 和 Gemini 均不显著 → judge-specific artifact 而非 universal bias\"\n      - \"casual-original subset 在所有条件下 ~50% → 全部 formality effect 可被 original preference 解释\"\n      - \"exp-002 additional benchmarks 不复制（p>0.15 across all new combos）\"\n      - \"exp-014 formality 主效应消失 (p>0.20)，仅 original status 显著\"\n    number_ranges:\n      binarized_likert_sign_p: [0.001, 0.04]\n      n_judges_pairwise_significant: [0, 1]\n      n_benchmarks_with_gap: [0, 1]\n      cross_judge_formal_win_rate: [0.48, 0.58]\n      cross_judge_pairwise_p_median: [0.05, 0.40]\n      casual_orig_formal_win_rate_best_judge: [0.45, 0.54]\n      logistic_formality_coef_p: [0.15, 0.60]\n      logistic_original_status_p: [0.001, 0.03]\n      projected_review_score: [2.8, 3.3]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      \"Likert Blindspot\" framing 不成立，需要根本性 reframe。两条备选路径：\n\n      路径 A（binarized Likert 显著 → C2 证伪）: Reframe 为方法论贡献 —\n      \"Pairwise comparison amplifies weak bias signals through rank-based \n      aggregation, but this amplification also inflates false positive risk. \n      Likert and pairwise should be treated as complementary detectors with \n      different sensitivity-specificity tradeoffs.\" \n      标题改为 \"Sensitivity-Specificity Tradeoffs in LLM Judge Bias Detection: \n      A Counterfactual Comparison of Likert and Pairwise Protocols\"。\n      C2 变成 \"pairwise 和 Likert 对 style bias 有不同检测特性\"（descriptive，非 prescriptive）。\n\n      路径 B（效应不泛化 / original preference 解释全部效应）: Reframe 为 \n      cautionary negative result — \"Counterfactual style intervention reveals \n      that apparent style bias in GPT-4o is confounded with original-text \n      preference; standard bias detection methods must control for this confound.\" \n      核心贡献变为 methodology warning + original preference 作为 hidden confound。\n      标题改为 \"Original-Text Preference Confounds Style Bias Detection in \n      LLM Judges: Evidence from Counterfactual Experiments\"。\n\n      两条路径均仍投 EMNLP Main / Findings。路径 A 定位 evaluation methodology，\n      路径 B 定位 negative result with methodological contribution。\n      C4 在此 tier 下反而更重要 — \"we show what NOT to do\" 本身就是 practical guidance。\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12716,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12716},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9da4503e-2b61-45f1-8689-f637435f2d27","uuid":"fcd20ae8-3fd0-485a-a1b2-b2769604ff83"}
